WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.967
The fine-tuning code is intentionally a skeleton because a complete training notebook needs several additional

00:00:06.967 --> 00:00:12.257
pieces. We begin with a pretrained tokenizer and AutoModelForSequenceClassification.

00:00:12.257 --> 00:00:19.087
Setting num_labels equal to two tells the classification head that our task has two output classes.

00:00:19.087 --> 00:00:25.882
From there, we need a labeled dataset, a train and test split, tokenization of the text column,

00:00:25.882 --> 00:00:31.652
training arguments, evaluation metrics, and a Trainer or custom training loop.

00:00:31.652 --> 00:00:36.442
During training, the model receives both tokenized input and labels.

00:00:36.442 --> 00:00:40.087
The loss compares predicted class scores with the true labels,

00:00:40.087 --> 00:00:43.522
and backpropagation updates the model parameters.

00:00:43.522 --> 00:00:50.727
For a beginner project, start with a small pretrained encoder such as DistilBERT so training is manageable.

00:00:50.727 --> 00:00:57.932
Always compare the fine-tuned model with a simple baseline such as logistic regression or TF-IDF.

00:00:57.932 --> 00:01:04.303
A Transformer is useful only if it provides enough improvement to justify the additional complexity and cost.
